WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.180
<v Mike>Touchdown Fever!

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:06.060
<v Joe>It's the muscle, the hustle, the chomp.

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:30.500
<v Mike>And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:31.580
<v Hosts>I'm Mike.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:33.660
<v Hosts>I'm Sean. And I'm Joe.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:38.260
<v Mike>Guys, it really is the muscle, the hustle, and the chomp of the NFL.

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:40.540
<v Mike>It's that time of the year again.

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:48.520
<v Mike>Obviously, recording this in real time, the Buffalo Bills remain undefeated.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:50.480
<v Sean>Don't you put that evil on us.

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:51.720
<v Sean>Don't make that happen.

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:54.820
<v Mike>Don't make it. Yeah, don't let them be undefeated.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:56.200
<v Mike>Let them lose a game or two.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:59.180
<v Sean>I just, you know, I think you actually made them lose.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:01.000
<v Sean>If we lost last week.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:02.480
<v Mike>Probably in the new stadium.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:08.300
<v Mike>No way. I've been hearing some shaky things about the new stadium recently.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:10.460
<v Sean>Yeah, they've got some obstructed views.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:12.100
<v Sean>They've got some...

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:15.100
<v Mike>Don't worry. The obstructed views will only be sold for concerts.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:16.500
<v Sean>Yeah, yeah. They say that.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:18.980
<v Mike>Yeah, that's reassuring.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:21.160
<v Mike>And then what's going on with the wind tunnels?

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:22.960
<v Sean>What about the wind tunnels?

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:25.760
<v Mike>I heard that they're pretty extreme.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:27.640
<v Sean>Huh. That sounds cool.

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:29.360
<v Joe>That sounds sick. Yeah, I like wind tunnels.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:31.160
<v Mike>Yeah, that sounds awesome. Right. That's like a perk.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:35.420
<v Mike>Yeah, yeah. It's a Buffalo living simulator.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:38.160
<v Sean>Yeah. They call us the Windy City.

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:39.580
<v Mike>Yeah. I did.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:45.320
<v Mike>I know that they don't call you guys that, but I did notice when I was in Buffalo for my

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:50.960
<v Mike>four years in college, it was always the windiest right before I got back to my dorm room.

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:53.880
<v Joe>Yeah, that's kind of a common weather thing for...

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:57.640
<v Mike>It was like right before I had to get to any particular door that was going to bring me

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:00.460
<v Mike>to warmth, it got significantly windier.

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:05.160
<v Sean>I think it's just literally that the campus is a wind tunnel in itself.

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:07.100
<v Sean>We're getting off track here.

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:10.140
<v Mike>I don't think we're getting off track, actually, because I was just about to ask you.

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:15.020
<v Mike>September 13th was the Bills' first game.

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:18.120
<v Mike>It was not a home game, so they didn't play at the new stadium.

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:19.960
<v Mike>They played against the Texans.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.100
<v Mike>Sean, do you remember the score of that game?

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:24.180
<v Sean>The score of the Texans game?

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:25.560
<v Mike>Yeah. Do you remember?

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:28.480
<v Mike>I mean, I know it was like eight days ago.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:31.140
<v Sean>Okay. Yeah, I can do this.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:36.500
<v Sean>Do you want me to predict the previous game before...

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:38.300
<v Joe>No, I just want you to tell me what the score was.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:40.540
<v Mike>Yeah, not predict, just remember. Yeah, just tell me what it was.

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:45.040
<v Sean>Because it already happened. It was 28 to 17 Bills.

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:54.820
<v Mike>Right. And now, you had a prop bet on it for Josh Allen over under two touchdowns.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:55.800
<v Mike>Do you remember?

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:57.640
<v Sean>If I won that?

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:00.840
<v Sean>Yeah. Yes, I definitely won that.

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:03.720
<v Mike>Especially if the score was 28, yeah.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:05.480
<v Mike>Yes, you did.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:07.980
<v Mike>Now, here's the other thing.

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:10.320
<v Sean>What else do you want me to predict?

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:21.300
<v Mike>Well, I think looking at the schedule here, okay, touchdown fever, I know that this one's hard to do because we don't record the episodes live.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:27.160
<v Mike>But yesterday was the Bills-Lions Thursday night game.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:31.220
<v Mike>Cool. What do you think the score was of that one?

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:34.600
<v Sean>I'm pretty sure that's going to be closer.

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:36.020
<v Sean>Probably more like...

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:40.200
<v Sean>No, you're setting me up to make a fool of myself, Mike.

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:42.660
<v Sean>The Bills are not guaranteed to be good this year.

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:47.060
<v Sean>We're probably not undefeated as this episode airs.

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:49.260
<v Sean>Everything sucks.

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:52.980
<v Sean>It was 31 to 5.

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:57.540
<v Sean>There was one field goal and a safety that we scored.

00:03:57.540 --> 00:03:59.800
<v Mike>Nice. So that's a scoreigami.

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:00.860
<v Mike>At least we can root for something.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:05.900
<v Mike>Yeah, exactly. I would like to see, actually, 31 to 5 a scoreigami.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:08.120
<v Mike>Meanwhile, you guys maybe talk about the game?

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:16.300
<v Joe>Yeah. Well, I don't want to gloss over the hustle, the bustle, the muscle, the chomp, or whatever that is.

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:18.180
<v Joe>Is that a football thing?

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:20.160
<v Sean>Oh, yeah. What is that?

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:23.320
<v Joe>It's not. I have no idea what the fuck they're talking about.

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:26.520
<v Joe>I was like, as soon as you said, oh, yeah, I was like, oh, my God, I sound so stupid.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:27.460
<v Joe>I don't know about the chomp.

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:29.540
<v Mike>Yeah, yeah. Last week, you didn't know about missiles.

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:32.320
<v Joe>He doesn't know about the chomp.

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:33.680
<v Joe>I don't know about the chomp.

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:37.260
<v Joe>I'm like, you said yes, I was like, shit, I sound like such a bleep.

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:38.660
<v Joe>I don't know about the chomp in football.

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:44.580
<v Sean>Can you imagine, like, pretending to be an American man and not knowing about the chomp?

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:47.160
<v Joe>The chain chomp.

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:48.140
<v Mike>What are they talking about then?

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:49.160
<v Joe>What is that?

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:51.180
<v Sean>I have no idea. Why is that written on the back of the box?

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:56.380
<v Joe>I googled it, and all I got was it's the famous marketing tagline of Touchdown Fever.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:02.920
<v Sean>The thing I will say about, like, my first impressions of this game, just going by the instruction manual,

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:06.720
<v Sean>is they expect that you have never heard of American football.

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:14.280
<v Sean>The manual is mostly teaching you about the sport of football, like the rules of the sport,

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:20.820
<v Sean>and has very little information about how they sort of video gamify it.

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:35.440
<v Sean>It's like a brief introduction to the control scheme and sort of explaining the info screens that happen between plays,

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:40.040
<v Sean>and then the rest of it is just, like, very base level, what does 4th down mean?

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:42.240
<v Sean>Which I found very endearing.

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:50.720
<v Mike>I just wanted to, you know, close the door on Scorigami and just say that four years ago,

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:54.720
<v Mike>the Cardinals beat the Texans 31-5.

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:56.100
<v Sean>Oh, wow. Shit.

00:05:56.300 --> 00:06:00.240
<v Mike>Yeah, it has happened, and pretty recently, too.

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:03.980
<v Mike>You know, Sean, thank you for bringing us back to the game.

00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:08.280
<v Mike>I appreciate everything that you said, and I don't disagree or...

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:09.460
<v Sean>Or was particularly listening.

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:11.900
<v Mike>Yeah, I don't want to correct anything you were saying.

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:16.060
<v Mike>I just wanted to point out that in addition to everything that you were saying about the game,

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:23.480
<v Mike>the first thing that I took away from this game after playing it for maybe, like, not even five minutes

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:25.100
<v Mike>was that I...

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:31.460
<v Mike>This is the first game that maybe made a football field feel long.

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:34.900
<v Sean>Oh, but it feels, like, longer than it should.

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:37.020
<v Mike>Right, longer than it should, no doubt.

00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:39.460
<v Mike>Like, it feels like the players are incredibly tiny,

00:06:39.460 --> 00:06:43.620
<v Mike>because I was so surprised that, like, some of my plays,

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:48.480
<v Mike>which felt like, you know, nice long passes that, like...

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:49.660
<v Sean>20-yard chunk play here.

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:52.780
<v Mike>Right, right, exactly. And then all of a sudden I see, like, third down.

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:55.480
<v Mike>I'm like, what the hell? I've been getting first down every time.

00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:56.780
<v Mike>Like, what are they talking about?

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:04.780
<v Mike>It's just dramatically different scale than Tecmo or John Elway or anything else we've played on this system.

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:10.200
<v Mike>So, and I'm sorry that John Elway was my second reference for an NES football game,

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:11.500
<v Mike>but, like, you know what I mean.

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:14.740
<v Mike>All the other ones are so zoomed out by comparison

00:07:14.740 --> 00:07:19.000
<v Mike>that I'm just not sure why the players aren't bigger here.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:26.600
<v Sean>The only thing that I can, like, possibly say is that you're playing as, like, toddlers.

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:36.160
<v Sean>Because each yard is, like, you could fit two people between each yard mark here.

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:39.040
<v Sean>So I think that's the only thing it could be.

00:07:39.700 --> 00:07:42.980
<v Sean>It's like you're playing football for ants.

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:48.500
<v Joe>Yeah. Yeah, and they do look, they're kind of got the, like, chibi look to them, too.

00:07:48.820 --> 00:07:53.080
<v Joe>Sort of reminds me a little bit of, um, and what is the, not Blades of Steel,

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:55.760
<v Joe>the other early hockey game we played?

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:57.220
<v Joe>It's just like NES hockey.

00:07:57.680 --> 00:07:59.660
<v Mike>Yeah. Yeah, ice hockey.

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:02.020
<v Joe>You got, like, a three different style player, whatever.

00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:07.340
<v Joe>This game overall did that, did what that game did for me for football.

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:11.660
<v Joe>Where, like, this feels a little more, like, arcade-y than some of the other games.

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:13.060
<v Joe>And just, like, yeah, you're smaller.

00:08:13.060 --> 00:08:20.580
<v Joe>Yes, it does feel like you're, you're going, you're, you're, the, the field feels way longer than it should.

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:30.200
<v Joe>But it also just, it just feels, like, a little more accessible and, I don't know, easy to, like, read football NES game, if that makes sense.

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:33.820
<v Mike>And they obviously, they did not get the NFL license here.

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:42.040
<v Mike>They, they're just, like, you know, giving you random, um, uh, cities to play as, which happened to also coincide with real NFL team locations.

00:08:42.040 --> 00:08:48.280
<v Mike>But you're not promised to be playing as, uh, you know, the Jets when you play as New York.

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:51.720
<v Mike>Uh, you're just playing as, uh, you know, blue team.

00:08:52.220 --> 00:08:57.840
<v Mike>And, uh, you can tell that the reason that they did that was not because, um, they couldn't afford the license.

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:07.080
<v Mike>It's because they're not using NFL, uh, they're not using the NFL rulebook because every player is allowed to grease themselves before they, uh, get on the field.

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:13.620
<v Mike>Because it's, have you ever noticed how many computer players can touch you before they successfully tackle you?

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:18.380
<v Mike>Like, so many times in this game did I think I was, like, oh, that plays over.

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:20.980
<v Mike>And then they just don't successfully tackle me.

00:09:21.080 --> 00:09:24.460
<v Mike>It's, like, the only thing I can come to the conclusion of is that we're all greased up.

00:09:24.860 --> 00:09:31.320
<v Joe>Yeah, there's a few, like, weird little, like, weird little things that, like, I guess make the game more playable but don't really make sense if you look at it.

00:09:31.340 --> 00:09:36.160
<v Joe>That being one of the other ones that, like, when you have the ball, I guess the weight of the ball slows you down.

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:40.120
<v Joe>Because you are always slower than anyone chasing you.

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:41.460
<v Sean>Yeah, it's, it's...

00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:43.240
<v Mike>Well, it is hard to run while holding a ball.

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:48.040
<v Sean>It's also an automatic interception if you throw the ball over somebody.

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:50.480
<v Sean>Um, I know that there are some instances...

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:59.000
<v Sean>Well, these people are infinitely tall. Yeah, I know there's some instances where, like, the game decides that you did throw the ball relatively high and it'll just, like, sort of go over them.

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:04.340
<v Sean>But it does not, it's not very good at, uh, showing that.

00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:17.580
<v Sean>And I think the biggest gripe that I have with this game, or, like, the most confusing thing about it, is that you're ostensibly choosing a play before each snap.

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:27.420
<v Sean>It says, like, short pass, long pass, uh, backs, which I guess just means a run play, um, field goal.

00:10:27.420 --> 00:10:30.780
<v Sean>Now, if you choose field goal, you will line up for a field goal.

00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:33.300
<v Sean>But everything else is just, like, a random play.

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:35.060
<v Sean>Like, there is no...

00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:36.440
<v Sean>Like, the...

00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:37.720
<v Sean>Did you notice this?

00:10:37.880 --> 00:10:44.540
<v Mike>That, like, where... Yes, no, yeah, you're just making the decision between a short and long pass, a QB sneak, and a regular running play.

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:53.860
<v Sean>Like, I have chosen long pass, and then both of my receivers just sort of hang out at the line of scrimmage, and I don't understand what that's about.

00:10:54.280 --> 00:10:55.900
<v Sean>Um, it...

00:10:55.900 --> 00:10:59.100
<v Sean>And then they're always lined up in random places.

00:10:59.560 --> 00:11:03.880
<v Sean>There's not any real way to, like, plan this out.

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:07.720
<v Mike>Um... Oh, like, there's no route to see ahead of time.

00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:09.240
<v Sean>Yeah, you have no idea what your player's gonna do.

00:11:09.580 --> 00:11:12.380
<v Sean>Yeah, exactly. And it'll be counterintuitive like that.

00:11:12.380 --> 00:11:18.600
<v Sean>And there's, like, it says backs, but sometimes your backs won't even be involved in the play.

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:21.520
<v Sean>Um, and you're still passing it.

00:11:21.880 --> 00:11:28.100
<v Sean>Now, one thing that they kind of did to offset this is that you can lateral the ball after a forward pass.

00:11:28.520 --> 00:11:33.280
<v Sean>Um, and I get that that is useful, but it is...

00:11:33.280 --> 00:11:41.380
<v Sean>It's a very strange mechanic to have in there when everything else is kind of, uh, is kind of paired back.

00:11:41.380 --> 00:11:50.580
<v Mike>Yeah, and also, you know, this whole game seems to be designed around a system where you could just, like, the strategy is just laterals, right?

00:11:50.700 --> 00:11:56.200
<v Mike>Just constantly just keep throwing laterals to one another, and that's more effective than any particular forward progress.

00:11:56.440 --> 00:12:04.640
<v Sean>Yeah. Well, the one thing, another note on just the fact that this isn't the NFL, uh, fun fact, that is, um...

00:12:04.640 --> 00:12:11.120
<v Sean>Uh, they have the two-point conversion, and the NFL didn't really have that, and I think until, like, the next year.

00:12:11.600 --> 00:12:14.020
<v Sean>So, that's a cute thing.

00:12:14.200 --> 00:12:20.820
<v Sean>But then you get the halftime show, and then there's obviously some correlation, because I played as Detroit.

00:12:21.460 --> 00:12:26.880
<v Sean>Um, the halftime show showed, you know, my cheerleaders come out with the mascot of a lion.

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:33.560
<v Sean>Um, but I was playing against Pittsburgh, and I think it was just, like, some tiger that came out.

00:12:34.060 --> 00:12:35.600
<v Mike>So, I'm not really sure what that was about.

00:12:35.600 --> 00:12:36.540
<v Mike>Yes, yes, yes.

00:12:36.860 --> 00:12:38.860
<v Mike>The, the... But Miami gets a dolphin.

00:12:39.120 --> 00:12:42.000
<v Joe>Miami does get a dolphin. Yeah, and Denver had some kind of horse-looking thing.

00:12:42.220 --> 00:12:44.960
<v Joe>Yes. I thought it was a cow at first, but...

00:12:44.960 --> 00:12:48.580
<v Joe>I thought it was a hippo at first, and then I realized, oh, it's Denver, so...

00:12:48.580 --> 00:12:54.660
<v Mike>Uh, you know, we were talking about the greased-up players, and that's true of both the...

00:12:54.660 --> 00:12:57.060
<v Mike>Like, I want to just say that, like, I did have...

00:12:57.060 --> 00:13:01.760
<v Mike>The computer did send, like, nine guys against me, and they all successfully missed me.

00:13:01.880 --> 00:13:06.100
<v Mike>But that can happen to you as well, because there is no tackle button.

00:13:06.200 --> 00:13:07.540
<v Sean>Yeah, you just sort of get in the way.

00:13:07.780 --> 00:13:10.700
<v Mike>You just collide and hope that they fall over.

00:13:10.920 --> 00:13:14.180
<v Joe>It's funny, I didn't realize there was no tackle button, because I always just hit the button.

00:13:14.180 --> 00:13:15.360
<v Joe>You think you're tackling, right, right.

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:16.620
<v Mike>You're probably hitting those buttons.

00:13:16.680 --> 00:13:21.340
<v Sean>Yeah, and sometimes it would work. We are all the little brother with the unplugged controller.

00:13:22.060 --> 00:13:29.580
<v Mike>Right. A and B are only used to select either the A or B player that it shows you at the start of the formation.

00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:31.960
<v Mike>From there, you're just that person.

00:13:32.120 --> 00:13:42.080
<v Sean>Which is really infuriating when, like, you have another player that has kind of an angle where you can make a tackle, but you can't switch to him.

00:14:00.200 --> 00:14:03.600
<v Mike>And I hate to say it, but I mean, like, that's the game.

00:14:03.860 --> 00:14:05.040
<v Mike>Like, that's what we're seeing.

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:11.740
<v Mike>So I guess now we need to start talking about, like, the presentation part, which we covered, that the field's enormous.

00:14:11.740 --> 00:14:13.600
<v Mike>But even, and we covered the halftime show.

00:14:13.760 --> 00:14:19.840
<v Mike>So I guess, like, you know, I guess we could talk maybe that the teams are always just blue versus pink.

00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:22.780
<v Sean>Or that the way that the score is presented.

00:14:22.780 --> 00:14:23.900
<v Sean>You can be blue versus gray.

00:14:24.700 --> 00:14:25.840
<v Mike>Oh, you can be.

00:14:25.900 --> 00:14:27.980
<v Mike>Okay, sorry. I thought it was always blue versus pink.

00:14:28.220 --> 00:14:30.100
<v Sean>Yeah, there's some different colors.

00:14:30.400 --> 00:14:31.280
<v Mike>There's some gray teams.

00:14:31.360 --> 00:14:48.520
<v Sean>Yeah. One of the most befuddling decisions is they don't flip the perspective when a team, when, like, you know, the away team takes possession of the ball.

